//? Title: Pareto-smoothed importance sampling leave-one-out
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Estimate leave-one-out predictive accuracy from a single posterior sample, stabilising the importance weights with a Pareto-smoothed tail and using the fitted tail shape as a reliability diagnostic

Leave-one-out cross-validation scores a Bayesian model by \(\mathrm{elpd}_{\mathrm{loo}}=\sum_i\log p(y_i\mid y_{-i})\). Refitting the model \(n\) times is expensive, but the leave-one-out posterior is the full posterior reweighted by \(1/p(y_i\mid\theta)\), so a single set of draws \(\theta^{(1)},\dots,\theta^{(S)}\) is enough in principle:

\[
p(y_i\mid y_{-i})\approx\frac{\sum_s r_{is}\,p(y_i\mid\theta^{(s)})}{\sum_s r_{is}},\qquad r_{is}=\frac{1}{p(y_i\mid\theta^{(s)})}.
\]

The raw ratios can have infinite variance when observation \(i\) is influential, and the estimate is then dominated by a few draws. Pareto-smoothed importance sampling (PSIS) fits a generalized Pareto distribution to the largest \(M\approx\min(0.2S,3\sqrt{S/r_{\mathrm{eff}}})\) ratios, replaces them by the fitted quantiles and truncates the result. The fitted shape \(\hat k\) doubles as a diagnostic: the estimate is reliable when \(\hat k\) is below \(\min(1-1/\log_{10}S,\,0.7)\), and a large \(\hat k\) means that observation \(i\) changes the posterior so much that the full posterior is a poor proposal for the leave-one-out posterior.

`pareto_smoothed_importance_sampling` smooths one vector of log ratios. `psis_loo` takes a draws-by-observations log-likelihood matrix and returns the totals \(\mathrm{elpd}_{\mathrm{loo}}\), \(p_{\mathrm{loo}}\) and LOOIC with standard errors, plus pointwise \(\hat k\) and effective sample sizes. `loo_compare` gives paired differences between models. The tests reproduce R's `loo` 2.9 to \(10^{-9}\), including per-observation relative efficiencies.

The check uses a Gaussian linear regression with known error SD and a flat prior. Its exact leave-one-out predictive density is normal and available in closed form, so each method can be scored against the truth. Each dataset has 40 points with \(x\sim U(-2,2)\) and 2000 exact posterior draws. There are three scenarios: clean data, one outlier (\(+5\) at an ordinary \(x\)), and one outlier at a high-leverage point \(x=6\). The table reports over 300 datasets the mean absolute error of the total \(\mathrm{elpd}_{\mathrm{loo}}\) for PSIS-LOO, for raw importance sampling (IS-LOO) and for WAIC, and how often the largest \(\hat k\) exceeded the threshold of 0.70.

```{cpp, id=psis, file="figures/psis_loo.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/bayes/diagnostics.hpp>
#include <datamunge/bayes/psis_loo.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/random.hpp>

#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <numbers>
#include <print>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;

double log_normal(double y, double mean, double var) {
    return -0.5 * std::log(2 * std::numbers::pi * var) - 0.5 * (y - mean) * (y - mean) / var;
}

struct Scores {
    double exact = 0, psis = 0, is = 0, waic = 0, max_k = 0;
    double is_i = 0, psis_i = 0, exact_i = 0;  // last observation
    std::vector<double> k, psis_error;
};

// y = b0 + b1 x + N(0, 1), flat prior: the posterior of b is N(bhat, (X'X)^{-1}).
Scores score(const std::vector<double>& x, const std::vector<double>& y,
             dm::random::SplitMix64& rng, std::size_t draws) {
    const std::size_t n = x.size();
    double sx = 0, sxx = 0, sy = 0, sxy = 0;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        sx += x[i], sxx += x[i] * x[i], sy += y[i], sxy += x[i] * y[i];
    }
    const auto solve = [](double a, double b, double c, double u, double v) {
        const double det = a * c - b * b;  // [[a b][b c]]^{-1} [u v]
        return std::array<double, 5>{(c * u - b * v) / det, (a * v - b * u) / det,
                                     c / det, -b / det, a / det};
    };
    const auto full = solve(double(n), sx, sxx, sy, sxy);
    // Cholesky of the posterior covariance [[V00 V01][V01 V11]].
    const double l00 = std::sqrt(full[2]), l10 = full[3] / l00;
    const double l11 = std::sqrt(full[4] - l10 * l10);
    std::vector<std::vector<double>> ll(draws, std::vector<double>(n));
    for (std::size_t s = 0; s < draws; ++s) {
        const double z0 = rng.normal(), z1 = rng.normal();
        const double b0 = full[0] + l00 * z0, b1 = full[1] + l10 * z0 + l11 * z1;
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i)
            ll[s][i] = log_normal(y[i], b0 + b1 * x[i], 1.0);
    }
    Scores out;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        const auto m = solve(double(n - 1), sx - x[i], sxx - x[i] * x[i], sy - y[i],
                             sxy - x[i] * y[i]);
        const double var = 1 + m[2] + 2 * m[3] * x[i] + m[4] * x[i] * x[i];
        out.exact += log_normal(y[i], m[0] + m[1] * x[i], var);
        double top = -1e300, sum = 0;  // raw IS: elpd_i = log S - logsumexp(-ll)
        for (std::size_t s = 0; s < draws; ++s)
            top = std::max(top, -ll[s][i]);
        for (std::size_t s = 0; s < draws; ++s)
            sum += std::exp(-ll[s][i] - top);
        out.is_i = std::log(double(draws)) - top - std::log(sum);
        out.is += out.is_i;
    }
    const auto loo = dm::bayes::psis_loo(ll);
    out.psis = loo.elpd_loo;
    out.waic = dm::bayes::waic(ll).elpd;
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        const auto m = solve(double(n - 1), sx - x[i], sxx - x[i] * x[i], sy - y[i],
                             sxy - x[i] * y[i]);
        const double var = 1 + m[2] + 2 * m[3] * x[i] + m[4] * x[i] * x[i];
        const double exact_i = log_normal(y[i], m[0] + m[1] * x[i], var);
        out.k.push_back(loo.pointwise[i].pareto_k);
        out.psis_error.push_back(loo.pointwise[i].elpd - exact_i);
        out.max_k = std::max(out.max_k, loo.pointwise[i].pareto_k);
        out.psis_i = loo.pointwise[i].elpd;
        out.exact_i = exact_i;
    }
    return out;
}

int main() {
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(17);
    const std::size_t n = 40, draws = 2000;
    const int reps = 300;
    const char* names[3]{"Clean data", "Outlier at ordinary x", "Outlier at x = 6"};
    std::vector<double> all_k, all_error;
    std::println("| Scenario | PSIS-LOO | IS-LOO | WAIC | Datasets with max k > 0.70 |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    for (int scenario = 0; scenario < 3; ++scenario) {
        double e_psis = 0, e_is = 0, e_waic = 0, high = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
            std::vector<double> x(n), y(n);
            for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
                x[i] = -2 + 4 * rng.uniform01();
                y[i] = 0.5 + 1.2 * x[i] + rng.normal();
            }
            if (scenario == 2)
                x[n - 1] = 6, y[n - 1] = 0.5 + 1.2 * 6 + rng.normal();
            if (scenario > 0)
                y[n - 1] += 5;
            const Scores sc = score(x, y, rng, draws);
            e_psis += std::abs(sc.psis - sc.exact) / reps;
            e_is += std::abs(sc.is - sc.exact) / reps;
            e_waic += std::abs(sc.waic - sc.exact) / reps;
            high += (sc.max_k > 0.7) / double(reps);
            if (scenario == 2)
                for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
                    all_k.push_back(sc.k[i]);
                    all_error.push_back(std::abs(sc.psis_error[i]));
                }
        }
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", names[scenario], e_psis,
                     e_is, e_waic, high);
    }

    // One leverage dataset, 300 independent posterior samples: the outlier's elpd_i.
    std::vector<double> x(n), y(n);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        x[i] = -2 + 4 * rng.uniform01();
        y[i] = 0.5 + 1.2 * x[i] + rng.normal();
    }
    x[n - 1] = 6, y[n - 1] = 0.5 + 1.2 * 6 + 5;
    double exact = 0, m[2]{}, q[2]{};
    for (int r = 0; r < reps; ++r) {
        const Scores sc = score(x, y, rng, draws);
        exact = sc.exact_i;
        const double v[2]{sc.psis_i, sc.is_i};
        for (int j = 0; j < 2; ++j)
            m[j] += v[j] / reps, q[j] += v[j] * v[j] / reps;
    }
    std::println("\nFixed leverage dataset, outlier's exact log p(y_i | y_-i) = {:.3f}:\n", exact);
    std::println("| Estimator | Mean over 300 posterior samples | SD |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: |");
    const char* est[2]{"PSIS-LOO", "IS-LOO"};
    for (int j = 0; j < 2; ++j)
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |", est[j], m[j], std::sqrt(q[j] - m[j] * m[j]));

    // Pointwise error against k-hat in the leverage scenario, averaged within k bins.
    std::vector<double> kmid, err_mean;
    for (double lo = -0.2; lo < 1.4; lo += 0.1) {
        double sum = 0;
        int count = 0;
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < all_k.size(); ++j)
            if (all_k[j] >= lo && all_k[j] < lo + 0.1)
                sum += all_error[j], ++count;
        if (count >= 5) {
            kmid.push_back(lo + 0.05);
            err_mean.push_back(std::log10(sum / count));
        }
    }
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(kmid, err_mean, "Mean |PSIS - exact| (log10)", {26, 90, 147}, 2)
        .points(kmid, err_mean, "", {26, 90, 147}, 5)
        .abline_v(0.7, {176, 72, 43}, 1)
        .y_limits(-2.8, 0.7)
        .title("Pointwise PSIS-LOO error by Pareto k")
        .x_label("Estimated Pareto k, leverage scenario (bins of 0.1; line: threshold 0.7)")
        .y_label("log10 mean absolute error of elpd_i")
        .size(620, 400);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/psis_loo.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Scenario | PSIS-LOO | IS-LOO | WAIC | Datasets with max k > 0.70 |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Clean data | 0.039 | 0.039 | 0.039 | 0.000 |
| Outlier at ordinary x | 0.059 | 0.061 | 0.057 | 0.037 |
| Outlier at x = 6 | 0.646 | 0.612 | 0.869 | 0.917 |

Fixed leverage dataset, outlier's exact log p(y_i | y_-i) = -11.598:

| Estimator | Mean over 300 posterior samples | SD |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| PSIS-LOO | -10.403 | 0.415 |
| IS-LOO | -10.683 | 0.771 |
\image(figures/psis_loo.svg)
// result_end

On clean data and with an outlier at an ordinary \(x\), all three estimates agree with exact leave-one-out to within a few hundredths of a log unit. This is Monte Carlo error that is negligible against the standard error of \(\mathrm{elpd}_{\mathrm{loo}}\). \(\hat k\) stays below the threshold in 96% or more of the datasets. A high-leverage outlier changes this. Leaving it out moves the regression line a lot, the full posterior is a poor proposal for the leave-one-out posterior, and every single-fit method misestimates that one term by about two-thirds of a log unit on average (WAIC by almost 0.9). The value of PSIS here is the diagnostic: the largest \(\hat k\) exceeded 0.70 in 92% of these datasets and in none of the clean ones. The figure shows that the pointwise error is about 0.003 for \(\hat k<0.3\) and starts to grow from about \(\hat k=0.4\). Above the threshold it levels off at around 0.5 to 0.8 log units, so \(\hat k\) also indicates how far an estimate can be trusted.

The second table isolates what the smoothing does. For a fixed dataset, the outlier's term is estimated from 300 independent posterior samples. Both estimators are biased upwards, because a finite sample rarely contains the draws that the leave-one-out posterior favours. Smoothing the tail halves the Monte Carlo SD (0.42 against 0.77) at the cost of a little more bias. The variance reduction makes estimates reproducible across reruns, and the bias that remains is flagged by \(\hat k\). PSIS does not make a bad proposal good. It makes the estimate stable and reports when the proposal is bad.

>> Practical guidance

- Pass the matrix of pointwise log likelihoods, with draws in rows and the units to be predicted in columns. For grouped data, sum the columns within a group to get leave-one-group-out.
- For MCMC output, pass per-observation relative efficiencies \(r_{\mathrm{eff}}=\mathrm{ESS}/S\) of \(p(y_i\mid\theta)\). They set the tail length and the effective sample size.
- Check `high_k`. For observations above the threshold, refit without them (exact LOO for those terms), use moment matching, or use K-fold cross-validation. Many high values also suggest a misspecified or very flexible model.
- Compare models with `loo_compare`, using the paired standard error of the difference rather than the separate standard errors. Treat differences of less than about 4 as small.
- Prefer PSIS-LOO to WAIC. They agree when all is well, and only PSIS-LOO warns when they do not.

>> References

- A. Vehtari, A. Gelman and J. Gabry (2017). Practical Bayesian model evaluation using leave-one-out cross-validation and WAIC. *Statistics and Computing* 27(5), 1413-1432. doi:10.1007/s11222-016-9696-4
- A. Vehtari, D. Simpson, A. Gelman, Y. Yao and J. Gabry (2024). Pareto smoothed importance sampling. *Journal of Machine Learning Research* 25(72), 1-58.
- J. Zhang and M. A. Stephens (2009). A new and efficient estimation method for the generalized Pareto distribution. *Technometrics* 51(3), 316-325. doi:10.1198/tech.2009.08017
